1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,924
The fire triangle.

2
00:00:02,644 --> 00:00:05,790
In episode two, we measured energy in
watt-hours.

3
00:00:05,970 --> 00:00:09,590
A kilogram of dry firewood holds about 4
kilowatt-hours of heat.

4
00:00:09,770 --> 00:00:14,935
Our stream's 245 watts would take about 17
hours to make that much.

5
00:00:15,115 --> 00:00:17,763
Wet wood wastes much of its heat boiling
off water.

6
00:00:17,943 --> 00:00:23,679
That's why, in England, firewood sold in
small bags must be certified Ready to
Burn:

7
00:00:23,679 --> 00:00:25,591
20 percent moisture or less.

8
00:00:26,811 --> 00:00:29,909
Fire lets that energy out, and it needs
three things.

9
00:00:30,089 --> 00:00:35,273
Heat, to start it. Fuel, to burn. And
oxygen, from the air.

10
00:00:35,453 --> 00:00:39,272
Take away any one side of the triangle,
and the fire goes out.

11
00:00:39,452 --> 00:00:43,331
Water takes away heat. A fire blanket
takes away oxygen.

12
00:00:43,331 --> 00:00:46,448
A cleared patch of ground starves it of
fuel.

13
00:00:46,448 --> 00:00:49,842
Never use water on a chip-pan or
electrical fire.

14
00:00:51,162 --> 00:00:54,875
A small flame can't heat a big log, so
build up in steps.

15
00:00:55,055 --> 00:00:59,722
Tinder first: dry grass, fluffed-up jute
twine, or cotton wool.

16
00:00:59,902 --> 00:01:03,736
Then sticks as thin as spaghetti, then
finger thick, then wrist thick,

17
00:01:03,736 --> 00:01:04,667
then bigger wood.

18
00:01:04,847 --> 00:01:10,767
A fire steel helps. Scrape it, and tiny
shavings of metal burn in the air as
sparks.

19
00:01:10,947 --> 00:01:14,108
And leave gaps, so oxygen can reach the
flames.

20
00:01:15,428 --> 00:01:17,596
Fire needs oxygen, and so do you.

21
00:01:17,596 --> 00:01:21,209
Never take a fire or a barbecue into a
tent or shelter.

22
00:01:21,389 --> 00:01:26,097
It gives off carbon monoxide, a gas you
can't see or smell, and it can kill.

23
00:01:26,277 --> 00:01:30,129
In England and Wales, the Countryside Code
says don't light fires,

24
00:01:30,129 --> 00:01:32,872
and only use barbecues where signs say you
can.

25
00:01:33,052 --> 00:01:37,410
Keep water close. To put a fire out, drown
it, stir it, drown it again,

26
00:01:37,410 --> 00:01:40,908
then hold the back of your hand close to
check it's cold.

27
00:01:42,628 --> 00:01:45,219
Remember: heat, fuel and oxygen.

28
00:01:45,399 --> 00:01:49,860
Take one away, and it goes out. Build
small to big, with dry wood.

29
00:01:50,580 --> 00:01:52,478
Next: how your body loses heat.
